  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nSatellite imagery shows widespread mid and high clouds covering \nthe region. These clouds are associated with the moisture plume \nfrom the weakening tropical cyclone Odalys. The moisture is being \ndrawn into SoCal by the southwest flow aloft. \n\nCirculation around a low pressure system moving in from the\nnorthwest will maintain the southwest flow over SoCal until the\nupper low moves east late Monday night. Meanwhile, the mass of\nclouds moving in from the southwest will continue to bring slight\nchances for light precipitation into Monday. Not much accumulation\nis expected and will most likely occur in the mountains of San\nDiego County. Even there, rain totals are unlikely to exceed one\ntenth of an inch. This low pressure system will also bring cooler\nweather with stronger onshore flow. Monday will likely be the\ncoolest day of the week, with daytime high temperatures near\nnormal at the coast, 5-7 degrees below normal in the inland \nvalleys and deserts, and as much as 10-15 degrees below normal in\nthe mountains. The onshore flow will be strongest on Monday with\nwesterly winds gusting 30-40 mph in the wind-prone areas of the\nmountains and adjacent deserts slopes. Onshore winds will weaken\nlate Monday.\n\nThe upper low will move east on Tuesday and a drying and warming\ntrend will begin as high pressure builds in from the west.\nNortherly flow aloft on the upstream side of the low will combine\nwith weak offshore pressure gradients to produce a relatively\nbrief period of offshore flow on Tuesday. The position of this low\npressure system will keep any moisture from Polo well to our south\nand east.\n\nThe dry and warm weather will continue into next weekend as the\nhigh pressure ridge settles over the Southwest. Daytime high \ntemperatures will return to above normal by Wednesday but the \nwarmest day will likely be Saturday. High temperatures on Saturday\nwill likely be 2 to 7 degrees above normal in the mountains and \ndeserts, and as much as 15 degrees above normal in the inland \nvalleys and coastal areas. The marine layer will be shallow and \nany low clouds will likely be restricted to the coastal areas \nduring the nights and mornings.",
